     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Deploying mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin together blocks every exfiltration path. Each exfiltration technique needs admin on the db, fileserver or backup host. Reaching any of those first needs auth_db, auth_fileserver or auth_backup. With mfa_dbadmin deployed, those three only work if the attacker holds admin_dc or admin_adfs. Both of those depend on auth_dc or auth_adfs, which mfa_admin blocks. So exfiltration becomes impossible even though the attacker can still reach the internal network, get stolen_dbadmin through the ciserver, and get stolen_admin. No single control is enough. Phishing is always available, so the attacker always gets a workstation foothold and stolen_user. This also bypasses mfa_user. Cutting off reach_internal would take three controls: segmented, patched:workstation and patched:laptop. With mfa:admin alone, stolen_dbadmin from the ciserver secrets leak still allows auth_db. With mfa:dbadmin alone, the attacker uses stolen_admin to run auth_dc and escalate_dc, and admin_dc bypasses the database MFA. Patching db, fileserver or backup does not help, because escalation also works with stolen_dbadmin. The minimum is therefore 2.\"}"
    }
   ],
